        ("Which statement guarantees the file is closed even if an exception occurs in the block?", ["fh.flush()", "The with statement", "Assigning None to fh", "fh.seek(0)"], 1, "The with statement closes the file through the context manager's __exit__, which runs even when an exception is raised."),
        ("What does fh.readlines() return?", ["One string with the entire file", "A list of lines each still ending in its newline", "A list of lines with newlines stripped", "A generator of stripped lines"], 1, "readlines returns a list and preserves each line's trailing newline character."),
        ("What does this print?\nwith open('f.txt', 'w') as fh:\n    fh.write('a')\n    fh.write('b')\nwith open('f.txt') as fh:\n    print(fh.readlines())", ["['ab']", "['a', 'b']", "['a\\n', 'b\\n']", "['ab\\n']"], 0, "Neither write adds a newline, so both characters land on one line and readlines yields the single element 'ab'."),
        ("Why pass encoding='utf-8' to open() instead of relying on the default?", ["It opens the file in binary mode", "It makes decoding independent of the machine's locale", "It creates the file when missing", "It speeds up reading"], 1, "Without an explicit encoding, open() uses the platform default, so the same bytes can decode differently on another machine."),
        ("Which call turns a Python dict into a JSON string without touching a file?", ["json.load(d)", "json.decode(d)", "json.dumps(d)", "json.dump(d)"], 2, "json.dumps returns a str, while json.dump writes to a file object and json.load reads from one."),
        ("What does Path('a/b.txt').suffix return?", ["'.txt'", "'b.txt'", "'txt'", "'a/b.txt'"], 0, "suffix includes the leading dot and returns only the final extension of the file name."),
